  "root_cause_analysis": {
    "finding": "Temperature Scaling's heuristic inflection point s0=0.78 gave higher probability predictions to specimens in the 0.75-0.85 similarity interval (mean predicted conf: 0.5737 for Temp vs 0.4819 for Platt). Because 75.8% of specimens in this interval were positive (true monuments), Temperature Scaling incurred lower squared error and log loss on positive monument specimens in this range. However, Temperature Scaling achieved this by fixing s0=0.78 manually rather than learning it from data, making its calibration dependent on the assumed threshold.",
    "mid_similarity_interval_0_75_to_0_85": {
      "total_specimens": 33,
      "positive_specimens": 16,
      "positive_rate": 0.4848,
      "platt_mean_predicted_prob": 0.4819,
      "temp_mean_predicted_prob": 0.5737,
      "platt_brier_in_interval": 0.2562,
      "temp_brier_in_interval": 0.2668
    }
  },
  "selection_verdict": {
    "rationale": "While Temperature Scaling achieved slightly lower fold-average ECE and Brier in CV (driven almost entirely by Fold 0), the paired t-test shows that the difference in ECE is NOT statistically significant (p = 0.7299 > 0.05). Furthermore, Temperature Scaling achieved this via a hand-tuned inflection point (s0=0.78), whereas Platt Scaling freely learned both slope and shift directly from data (crossover s0=0.8387). To determine which model generalizes best under rigorous scientific evaluation, both models will be frozen and compared on the model-selection split (Split B) and the final independent holdout (Split C)."
  }
